''We're too late for spring cleaning, but just in time for a Summer Sweep-Up! Strap in because we've got a lot to talk about!''

==Blog Update - Tuesday, June 9th==
It's been great to see such a positive reception to our initial Summer Sweep-Up proposal!

We wanted to check back in with you to address some common talking points, make some changes to our initial offering, and provide clarity on some aspects that we felt could have been clearer.

Let's get straight into it!

===Soulreaper Axe===
Our initial proposal here was to lower the maximum stack count on the axe, but increase the damage bonus offered by each stack, so that you'd spend less time ramping up for your damage.

On the surface of things this was fine, but many of you felt as though it doesn't address the core reasons that the Soulreaper Axe feels annoying to use, namely the self-damage and having to ramp back up between kills or during intermission phases for some content (ironically, our proposal made losing stacks even more detrimental than their current iteration).

So we'd like to instead propose striking our initial stack-related changes from the record and adjusting the Soulreaper Axe as follows:
* Gains +4 Strength bonus, this is unchanged from our initial proposal.
* Stack limit remains at 5, with 6% damage bonus gained per stack.
* Stacks take 50 ticks to start decrementing, up from 20 ticks.
* Stacks no longer drain HP whatsoever.
* Special attack now gains 12% accuracy per stack, up from 6% (60% at max stacks)
* Special attack now gains 6% minimum hit per stack (30% at max stacks)

This should result in more consistent uptime at max potential, while also reducing how often you send yourself to an early respawn!

===Inquisitor Gear Changes===
We had a handful of typos or errors in last week's post, so we'd like to reiterate the changes here using the correct numbers to make sure that everybody's on the same page.

The aim here is to bring Inquisitor's offensive statting in-line with Oathplate, while also reducing the reliance on the full set + Inquisitor's Mace combo, instead hoping to make each piece shine brighter individually. However, our numbers yesterday resulted in weakening Crush at the top end, which was not our intent, so we've made a few more tweaks here!
* Inquisitor's Great Helm gains +2 Crush accuracy and +2 Strength bonus, to match its Oathplate equivalent.
** Additionally, provides a +0.5% bonus to Crush damage and Crush accuracy. This boost is no longer increased by a set effect or when using the Inquisitor's Mace specifically.
* Inquisitor's Hauberk gains +4 Crush accuracy, to match its Oathplate equivalent.
** Additionally, provides a +1% bonus to Crush damage and Crush accuracy, up from +0.5%. This boost is no longer increased by a set effect or when using the Inquisitor's Mace specifically.
* Inquisitor's Plateskirt gains no Crush accuracy or Strength, since it already matches its Oathplate equivalent.
** Additionally, provides a +1% bonus to Crush damage and Crush accuracy, up from +0.5%. This boost is no longer increased by a set effect or when using the Inquisitor's Mace specifically.
* Inquisitor's Mace gains +7 Strength bonus (up from +5 in our initial proposal) and +7 Crush accuracy. This guarantees a max hit in all setups, and might add +2 max hits in some setups.

To top this off, we'll be removing any negative Stab or Slash bonuses from Inquisitor's armour pieces.

These numbers should shake out more-or-less as they currently do, with some better accuracy in the mix and better future-proofing, while also not effectively requiring the full set for the armour to feel worth using!

===Other Gear Changes===
Before moving on to other topics, we've a few more points to make!
* Tonalztics of Ralos were something that many of you felt could do with a little extra juice. While we don't think the item itself is bad, we do think it could do with more use-cases to make it stand out. To make those use-cases easier to develop (since there's a fairly complex relationship between Defence level, Magic level, Light Ranged Defence and Crush Defence to figure out whether you'd use this or an Elder Maul), we'd like to make some small improvements:
** Special attack buffed to reduce opponent's Defence by 12.5% of its Magic level per hit, up from 10%.
** Special attack buffed to grant +50% increased accuracy.
* Suggestions relating to the Ghrazi Rapier have been a hot topic lately, particularly the idea of making it a 3-tick weapon with its own discernible niche. We're not inherently opposed to the idea, but aren't confident we have the time to get these numbers right in a way that's safe long-term - viable 3-tick weapons benefit disproportionately from any increases in Strength bonus from other gear, meaning this isn't something that we're keen to explore right now just because it might be satisfying. Naturally some weapons like the Scythe of Vitur also benefit disproportionately from Strength bonus, but we feel this is less of an issue for megarares.
* We'll be expanding the scope of the Ghommal's Lucky Penny passive effect so that it can preserve charges from the Amulet of Blood Fury. Admittedly the plan is that this won't be an item post-Sweep-Up, but this seemed the most appropriate place to mention this change.
* Despite it being a nerf, most of you seemed understanding of the change we'd like to make to Burning Claws. Some players pointed out that they enjoyed a niche use in CM Chambers of Xeric for Ice Demon, which helped move away from a relatively tedious Magic Cape Spellbook Swap meta. We'll be exploring an increase to Ice Demon's demonbane vulnerability to see whether or not we can still have the Burning Claws occupy this space.
* We've adjusted our Rosewood Blowpipe proposal to remove the reduced accuracy on the Special Attack. This is a small conundrum for us, since the Doom of Mokhaiotl finally gave Dragon Knives their first ever PvM-related use-case, and leaving the Rosewood Blowpipe's Special Attack untouched while also allowing Rune Darts effectively removes this use-case instantly. We appreciate that this isn't the biggest issue facing the game, but do quite like seeing niche items find uses and are hesitant to throw them away for an item whose primary function is a Light Ranged DPS weapon as a stepping stone to the Toxic Blowpipe.
* While not strictly gear-related, we've updated the Crystal Shard numbers in the Gauntlet Prep Changes segment of the main Sweep-Up blog so that they're accurate - apologies for any confusion caused by errors in the initial post.

===Other Skilling Changes===
We don't have a massive amount to share here, so we'll keep things brief!
* Some players asked what the point in a Molch pearl refund at Aerial Fishing is if there's nothing to purchase, so we'd like to add Shark Lures to the shop here for a reliable 'sink' if you fancy spending your excess.
* We're opting not to adjust the recipe for the Energy Potion. Many of you pointed out that Harralanders are much more plentiful than Guams past a certain point, and it didn't seem as though anybody was particularly overjoyed with this specific change anyway, so we'll avoid rocking the boat too much - your Perilous Moons herb stacks are safe!
* We'll look to add another Agility shortcut to the mix by way of a stepping stone across the River Lum, just south of the Champion's Guild.

===Birdhouses===
To our surprise, we didn't see as much discussion about our desire to nerf Birdhouses' XP offering as we'd expected. Perhaps that's because you agree that it's justified, or were more focused on other changes, or the 'vagueness' of the section didn't give you much to go on. In an effort to make things feel a little more 'real' and drum up some proper discussion, we'd like to put some numbers in front of you for what we've got in mind. We'd like to reiterate that we are still not 100% committed to making a change here, but do believe that repositioning this timegated content that feels closer to Farming than it does to Hunter would enable us to improve the feel of Hunter progression for players in future.

With that said, here's what we're provisionally working with:
* Prospective Bird Nest Changes (buffs)
** Number of rolls for nests from all tiers of birdhouse lowered from 10 to 5.
** Success rate on any given roll for a nest is tripled.
** These changes amount to a ~50% increase in the number of nests obtained, and should make the quantity you obtain more consistent and less 'spiky' so that you're not overfilling your inventory on some runs but leaving empty-handed on others.
* Prospective XP Changes (nerfs)
** Regular birdhouse XP decreased by 60%.
** Oak birdhouse XP decreased by 60%.
** Willow birdhouse XP decreased by 60%.
** Teak birdhouse XP decreased by 60%.
** Maple birdhouse XP decreased by 55%.
** Mahogany birdhouse XP decreased by 50%.
** Yew birdhouse XP decreased by 40%.
** Magic birdhouse XP decreased by 15%.
** Redwood birdhouse XP unchanged.

Naturally at the lower tiers this is - as we said in the initial blog - a big reduction. It might be the case that the other buffs and improvements to early-game Hunter are insufficient and we'd need some numerical improvements to things like butterfly hunting to pick up the slack at certain stages of progression, but the best way for us to nail down these other lacking areas is the invite more discussion, for better or worse!

===Chambers of Xeric, Challenge Mode & Prayer Scrolls===
This one also drummed up a little less chatter than we'd anticipated, so we'd like to bring the topic of prayer scrolls back up.

Based on our early read, it feels like narrowing down our list of possible approaches would be a stronger place to start from, so we'd like to whittle our options down to two!

====Option 1: Unique re-roll mechanic (including some clarifications)====
This was our more complicated option, but one that does solve most of the problems or address most of our concerns. It seemed as though many of you agreed that it does solve those problems, but did have issues with how a system like this feels alongside concerns about us setting a precedent for options like this. We'll start with a couple of clarifications:
* The aim here was to allow people to re-roll anything, not just prayer scrolls. This means that any unique whose value is below the 'average' re-roll value should (in theory) be sunk more frequently and appreciate in value slightly.
* If we did pursue a system like this, we'd like to make it feel a little more 'in-world' than just clicking a button on an interface, which we agree would feel wrong.
* We feel CoX's issues are unique and this is a relatively bespoke solution that we wouldn't expect to just roll out willy-nilly to any piece of content that was struggling, we don't expect this to set a precedent on its own.

Clarifications aside, the numbers here are considerably more complicated (we'd recommend checking out this post for a neat look at various potential re-roll rates and scenarios though).

To make things easier, the re-roll rate that we're toying with at the moment is a 1 in 5, meaning you'd have a 20% chance of re-rolling your item into another with the initial item's weighting removed from the loot table. This is a fairly generous option in terms of the number of non-prayer scroll uniques that it would likely introduce, which we're comfortable moving ahead with due to the trade-off involved and the likelihood of increasing the value of cheaper uniques in the process.

We did see a handful of comments that re-rolling and getting nothing can admittedly feel quite bad, but our expectation is that for people willing to re-roll in the first place, the unique that they're re-rolling might as well be 'nothing' already. We'd also like to say that even a 'failed' re-roll would instead provide a roll on the regular loot table, so you'd never be leaving entirely empty-handed.

If this one's not resonating with you, then perhaps:

====Option 2: Increase purple rate by 5%, decrease combined scroll weighting from ~58% to ~56%====
As we see it, the primary issues that many have with Chambers of Xeric are:
* Issue #1: The general 'time-to-complete' for uniques is extremely long.
* Issue #2: The ratio of prayer scrolls to non-prayer scroll uniques feels bad.

These numbers might not look like much, but they mean that non-prayer scroll uniques become 10% more common, and you'll be rolling on that table slightly more frequently! This tackles both to a small extent but should aid the feel of Chambers of Xeric completions, even though you'll still see a fair few prayer scrolls.

We're also aware of the feeling that CoX has too many uniques on its table and saw some suggestions to try and move various uniques off. This isn't something that we're inherently opposed to, but it's not a solution that we think we could work through and deliver confidently in a reasonable timeframe this year. Additionally, last time we asked similar for moving the Dragon Claws onto their historic source (Tormented Demons), opinion did lean towards moving them but not by a massive margin, which doesn't give us much confidence in being able to move multiple uniques to other sources in a way that satisfies most players.

===Closing Thoughts===
As expected, there's been a bunch of discussion around things that haven't made an appearance this year like the Corporeal Beast or Mastering Mixology. While we can't make any commitments, we've said in a handful of replies to players that we are exploring whether or not we could deliver Sweep-Up styled updates at a greater frequency if we have enough interesting things to do, so even if your favourite suggestion hasn't surfaced yet there's a high chance that it's on our backlog waiting for its moment to shine.

That's all we've got for our small blog update, so we'll make our exit and leave you to mull over what we've shared while undoubtedly keeping up with the Deadman All-Stars action!

